WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.868
-It's so nice to see you, buddy.

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:02.901
I can't even sit in this.

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:08.400
[ Laughter ]

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.734
I knew I'd make you laugh.
How are you, bud?

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:13.567
-I'm good. How are you?
-I'm fantastic.

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:15.734
Thank you so much for coming
back on "The Tonight Show."

00:00:15.734 --> 00:00:18.400
I appreciate it.
I always love having you here.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.200
I wanted to wear
something special for you

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.567
because I know you.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.934
I know it's a big deal
to win three Michelin stars.

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:29.801
Because, I mean, this is --
it's a plant-based restaurant.

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:34.567
-You know, when I was a kid
and first started out cooking,

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.701
getting three Michelin stars
seemed like a dream

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:41.334
so big, like, scary,
scary to dream of.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:43.767
It seemed unreachable.

00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:47.100
And of course, 12 years ago,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.100
eventually,
we reached that goal,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.234
and it's such
a amazing honor,

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:57.033
and it's really meaningful
to the team and everyone.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.734
But that we reached
this -- Michelin

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:02.567
has created the rules
for fine dining.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.501
They've been around
for over 100 years,

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:10.267
and to reach that now
without using meat or fish

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:12.901
is something I never
thought was possible.

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:16.601
And it's sort of a marker that
there is change.

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:19.000
Like, with the restaurant,
we can create change.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.067
-Yeah.
When did you get the idea

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:24.567
that you were going to switch
completely to plant-based?

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:26.434
-It was really
during the pandemic.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:29.701
You know,
we turned the restaurant

00:01:29.701 --> 00:01:31.000
into a community kitchen,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.434
which you know. We started
feeding people in need.

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:39.901
And even though it was so scary
financially and we lost our team

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:42.834
and it was such a challenging
time for restaurants

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:44.601
and other businesses,

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:48.300
but I found
this whole new meaning.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.200
Before that, it was
all about winning awards,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.267
getting accolades,
and then during the pandemic,

00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:57.734
it felt like,
wow, there is a higher purpose

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:02.567
and I felt more connected
with the language of food.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:05.934
I felt more connected
with New York City.

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:09.434
And so when it was time
to reopen the restaurant,

00:02:09.434 --> 00:02:11.067
it just was clear
that I didn't want

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:14.601
to open the same restaurant
as before.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.200
And this is kind of
how this book came to be.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.033
-Explain because "Eat More
Plants" is the name of the book,

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:23.634
and it is gorgeous, by the way.
-Thank you.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:24.901
-By the way, I don't know
if you know this,

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:26.567
but it smells like crayons.

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:30.033
-I know. It's so nice. It does.
-It really does.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:34.834
It is like -- But it's your
personal journal and painting.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:38.400
-It was never
really meant to be a book,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.601
but it was sort of like I've
been journaling my whole life

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:47.400
and I believe to speak things
into existence,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.834
but even before I speak them
into existence,

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:56.400
I sort of like meditate
on these ideas in my journals.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.467
-Yeah.
-And during the pandemic,

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:01.167
it was really
sort of this meditation

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:03.000
on all kinds of vegetables

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.501
and sort of -- there was also
some thoughts in there

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.534
about reopening the restaurants.

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:09.434
-Yeah, there's a couple
of things in here

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:12.234
I was reading and I go,
"Oh, this is pretty personal,"

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:13.934
where you're like,
"Is this going to work?

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:15.801
Are people going to
come to this?"

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:18.634
-It is really personal.
And when I look at it now,

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:22.000
I remember those times
in the pandemic,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.300
it was so scary.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.400
And, you know, it's not
just an artistic endeavor,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.767
but it's also a livelihood.

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:31.701
So, like,
we need guests to come.

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:34.901
And thankfully they have come.

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:39.167
But during the pandemic, I was
really laying awake at night

00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:40.734
and didn't know what's going
to be next.

00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:43.634
-If you had a future.
Well, how does this book --

00:03:43.634 --> 00:03:44.968
how do you meet someone

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:47.234
that's going to put
this book out?

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.734
-So --
-Who did this?

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.601
-It was a friend of mine,
Roni Horn,

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:54.400
who is this amazing artist
who brought in her friend,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.300
who is this publisher
in Germany

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.968
who creates some of
the most beautiful art books.

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:01.634
And they came into dinner
at Eleven Madison Park,

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:06.133
and he was so
taken by the experience.

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:08.801
And he asked about
the creative process.

00:04:08.801 --> 00:04:10.501
And I was telling him
about my journal,

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:13.667
and then he said,
"Can I come see them?"

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.534
And I said, "Well,
they're not really,

00:04:15.534 --> 00:04:17.734
you know, for people to see.
They're very personal."

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:19.801
But then, sure enough,
he showed up.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:21.133
His name is Gerhard Steidl.

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:24.868
He's an icon
in the publishing world.

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:27.634
But he showed up
and he saw the journals,

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:30.767
and he said, "I would love
to do a book with you."

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.234
And that's how it came to be.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.534
-It's gorgeous.
And it makes me happy.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:37.367
And it makes me think

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.701
that there's
a future in this, buddy.

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:41.434
You're the greatest and always
doing something different.

00:04:41.434 --> 00:04:44.234
-You make me laugh.
Thank you.

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:47.934
-Chef Daniel Humm, everybody.
"Eat More Plants"

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:51.367
is available now
at elevenmadisonhome.com.

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.400
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.634
Stick around, everybody.

